    "memoText": "\n \n                         VETO MESSAGE - No. 330\n \nTO THE ASSEMBLY:\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n \nAssembly Bill Number 5931-A, entitled:\n \n    \"AN  ACT to amend the executive law, in relation to establishing the\n      New York state interagency coordinating council for  service-disa-\n      bled  veterans,  defining the powers and duties of the council and\n      requiring an annual report by the council  to  the  governor,  the\n      temporary president of the senate and the speaker of the assembly\"\n \n    NOT APPROVED\n \n  This  bill would add a new Executive Law Section 368 to create the New\nYork State Interagency Coordinating Council for Service-Disabled  Veter-\nans,  consisting of ten different agency heads and nine service-disabled\nveteran representatives appointed by the Governor and the State Legisla-\nture.\n \n  While this bill is well-intentioned, the  existing  State  Council  on\nVeterans  and  the Department of Veteran's Affairs already performs this\nfunction. In March 2014, I signed Executive Order No.  130  that  estab-\nlished  the  New  York  State  Council on Veterans, Military Members and\nTheir Families (\"Council on Veterans\"). The Council on Veterans develops\nrecommendations for a comprehensive strategy  to  address  the  multiple\nneeds  of  veterans and their families in adjusting to post-service life\nthrough a collaboration of federal, State and local benefits.  Moreover,\nmany  of  the  functions  of  the  proposed council are performed by the\nfederal government. In 2010, identical legislation was vetoed (see  Veto\nNo. 6757 of 2010) for similar reasons and those same reasons still apply\ntoday.\n \n  The  needs  of  veterans  who become disabled in the course of serving\ntheir country must be met, which is why my  administration  works  tire-\nlessly to see that the comprehensive strategy recommended by the Council\non  Veterans  is implemented. Additionally, the staff at the Division of\nVeterans Affairs,  as  well  as  numerous  veterans'  services  officers\nthroughout  the State, are available to assist these veterans in obtain-\ning federally available services. For the above stated reasons, I cannot\nsupport this bill.\n \n  The bill is disapproved.                    (signed) ANDREW M. CUOMO\n \n                               __________\n",
    "signedDate": null,
    "signer": "ANDREW M. CUOMO",
    "vetoNumber": 330,
    "vetoType": "STANDARD",
    "year": 2018
   }
